<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Colony of Singapore</h1><p class="st-meta">1946 CE – 1963 CE · capital Singapore</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">British crown colony created when Singapore was separated from the Straits Settlements, and the stage for its path to self-rule.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> dissolved the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/straits-settlements">Straits Settlements</a> in 1946, it kept <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/singapore">Singapore</a> apart from the new <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/malayan-union">Malayan Union</a> because of its Chinese majority and its value as a naval base. The Colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> was established on 1 April 1946 under a governor.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The post-war years brought labour unrest, communist activity and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malayan-emergency">Malayan Emergency</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rendel-constitution">Rendel Constitution</a> of 1955 created a partly elected legislature, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-marshall">David Marshall</a> became chief minister. Full internal self-government came in 1959 as the State of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/singapore">Singapore</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lee-kuan-yew">Lee Kuan Yew</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-action-party">People's Action Party</a> as prime minister, while <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> kept defence and foreign affairs.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lee-kuan-yew">Lee</a> pursued merger with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/federation-of-malaya">Malaya</a> to end colonial rule and gain an economic hinterland. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/singapore">Singapore</a> joined <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/malaysia">Malaysia</a> on 16 September 1963, but political and racial tensions led to separation in 1965, creating the independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/colony-of-singapore.jpg" alt="Colony of Singapore" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Fry1989 eh? · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Singapore_(1952%E2%80%931959).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1946 CE – 1963 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Singapore</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>David Marshall, Lee Kuan Yew</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism, Chinese folk religion, Sunni Islam, Christianity, Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English, Malay, Chinese, Tamil</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/singapore">Singapore</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Crown Colony of Singapore, State of Singapore</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1954&amp;focus=colony-of-singapore" aria-label="Colony of Singapore on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7563 2129 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Colony of Singapore lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7883 2385L7883 2385M7883 2385L4997 651M7883 2385L7823 2316M7883 2385L7883 2385"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7883 2385h0M4997 651h0M7823 2316h0M7883 2385h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7883 2385h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7883 2385h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Singapore. <a href="/atlas?year=1954&amp;focus=colony-of-singapore">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
